(University Study Primer - Applied Machine Learning Series) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. From Raw Text to Tokens) Tj
T*
(Large Language Models \(LLMs\) do not read words the way humans do. Instead, they) Tj
T*
(break text into smaller units called tokens. A token can be a whole short word,) Tj
T*
(a piece of a longer word, or even punctuation. On average, 100 tokens equals) Tj
T*
(about 75 English words. Tokenization lets a model handle rare words, code, and) Tj
T*
(multiple languages using a fixed vocabulary of around 50,000 to 100,000 subwords.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2. Embeddings: Turning Words Into Coordinates) Tj
T*
(Once text is split into tokens, each token is converted into a vector of numbers) Tj
T*
(called an embedding. Words with similar meanings or contexts sit closer together) Tj
T*
(in this high-dimensional space. For example, the vectors for university and) Tj
T*
(college point in nearly the same direction, allowing mathematical comparison.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3. The Transformer Architecture and Self-Attention) Tj
T*
(Introduced in 2017, the Transformer replaced slow sequential processing with) Tj
T*
(parallel computation. Its core mechanism is self-attention, which scores how) Tj
T*
(strongly every token in a sentence relates to every other token. In the sentence) Tj
T*
(The battery died because it was old, self-attention links the pronoun it back) Tj
T*
(to battery rather than died, preserving long-range context accurately.) Tj
T*
ET
