(Page 3: Hallucinations, Grounding, and Practical Use) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
0 -28 Td
16 TL
(7. Understanding Hallucinations) Tj
T*
(Because language models predict probable next tokens rather than looking up) Tj
T*
(verified facts in a database, they can occasionally produce confident-sounding) Tj
T*
(errors known as hallucinations. Hallucinations are most common when asking for) Tj
T*
(obscure citations, exact private figures, or facts absent from the prompt.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(8. Grounding Answers in Source Documents) Tj
T*
(Retrieval-Augmented Generation \(RAG\) and document grounding solve this issue) Tj
T*
(by supplying exact excerpts from a trusted PDF inside the model prompt and) Tj
T*
(instructing the model to answer strictly from that evidence. When a detail is) Tj
T*
(missing from the source text, a well-designed system explicitly informs the) Tj
T*
(user instead of guessing.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(9. Key Takeaways for Students and Researchers) Tj
T*
(First, treat AI summaries as rapid study maps, not replacements for primary) Tj
T*
(verification. Second, break dense textbooks into manageable chunks when asking) Tj
T*
(questions. Third, test comprehension actively using self-quizzes and flashcards) Tj
T*
(rather than passively re-reading highlighted paragraphs.) Tj
T*
ET
